<article class="env-lemma" id="035D">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/035D">Lemma <span data-tag="035D">29.45.14</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to Y$ and $g : Y \to Z$ be morphisms. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $g \circ f$ is finite and $g$ separated then $f$ is finite. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $g \circ f$ is integral and $g$ separated then $f$ is integral. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $g \circ f$ is finite (resp. integral) and $g$ separated. The base change $X \times _ Z Y \to Y$ is finite (resp. integral) by Lemma <a href="/tag/01WL" data-tag="01WL">29.45.6</a>. The morphism $X \to X \times _ Z Y$ is a closed immersion as $Y \to Z$ is separated, see Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/01KT" data-tag="01KT">26.21.11</a>. A closed immersion is finite (resp. integral), see Lemma <a href="/tag/035C" data-tag="035C">29.45.12</a>. The composition of finite (resp. integral) morphisms is finite (resp. integral), see Lemma <a href="/tag/01WK" data-tag="01WK">29.45.5</a>. Thus we win. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>